WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:07.940
LAURA BANDURA: Hello everyone.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.620
My name is Laura Bandura, and
I'm a research geophysicist

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.000
at Chevron.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.880
Some of you may remember that
I was on stage at Google Next

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.110
last July.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:19.690
Back then, I talked about
how to make sense out of way

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:21.400
too much data.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.680
We were using Google
Cloud's AutoML Vision

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.260
API to analyze the
content of thousands

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:31.350
of geoscience documents and
to make them searchable.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:34.170
My goal then was to describe
how we could quickly

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.800
access and utilize the work
documented by thousands

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.170
of Chevron employees
in the form of field

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.920
reports, seismic recordings,
core samples, lab

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.610
analyses, environmental
studies, and much more.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:51.990
With Google Cloud, we got it
in one place, digitized it,

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.270
and through the power
of machine learning

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.845
gave this data new life.

00:00:56.845 --> 00:00:58.470
Today, I want to tell
you how far we've

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.420
come with that
project and how we've

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.390
added tools to do something
far more valuable--

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.720
creating a new way of
consuming information

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.600
by a rich and diverse
team of people

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.180
and acting faster to
achieve our business goals.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.600
The energy business literally
powers the modern world.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.030
For someone like me,
part of the excitement

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:23.210
is understanding how we
use geology and geophysics

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:26.030
to understand the
subsurface of the Earth.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.580
In my work, we use
seismic waves to probe

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.680
the subsurface geology.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:34.130
Those measurements are then
used to create detailed pictures

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:36.920
of what the Earth looked
like deep into the past

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.880
and also to help us determine
where oil and gas are located.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.360
Here we show an example of a
modern river system containing

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:48.250
channel features that have
similar dimensions and patterns

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.090
to features that
we tried to find

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:53.740
in the subsurface
for exploration.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:57.090
And one of these
examples is this image

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.820
of a submarine
canyon system that we

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.910
found within seismic data.

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:05.200
But the excitement
doesn't stop there.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.850
The next step is to connect our
interpretations of these images

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.730
to the value chain and
to ultimately impact

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:15.190
how we produce oil and gas.

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:18.860
For an integrated energy
company like Chevron

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.340
that means working with
multiple teams globally

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:24.200
that include research
scientists, engineers,

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.450
and analysts.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.630
Some of us are working in
offices interpreting data,

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:31.930
and others are out in the
field conducting operations.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:35.290
Communicating with our peers
across geographic and language

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:38.380
boundaries and across
subject matter domains

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:40.060
is nothing new to Chevron.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:43.750
In fact, we've done this
very well for over a century.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:47.380
Over time however,
information can become siloed.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:51.760
What if we could create
some common digital spaces

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.070
where all teams
could look at all

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.170
of the available
information to support

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:57.820
the work that they are doing?

00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:59.770
What if all of
Chevron's experts could

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:03.310
have the best data available
at all times, regardless

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.072
of where they are
located or what

00:03:05.072 --> 00:03:06.280
their technical specialty is?

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:12.780
How would we organize
something like that?

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.520
And the answer is to take
things back to basics.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:17.960
And for us, that is the
earth and to build it up

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:19.550
in a whole new way.

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:21.830
On Google Cloud,
we've created a system

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:26.150
that digitizes text, tables, and
images, all sorts of notations

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:27.560
and data readings.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.860
We've made everything
searchable through metadata that

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:32.780
was automatically generated.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:37.850
With AutoML, we utilize custom
geologic labels to tag images

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:42.540
and use knowledge graphs
to summarize field reports.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.300
With natural language
processing and translation,

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:50.190
we created an enormous index
of our data and documents

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:53.310
that is now searchable
in any language.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:55.200
This means that
data from years ago

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.840
can be combined with
more recent findings

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.000
or matched against
technology improvements

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.050
that we've made in field
development planning and safety

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:07.470
or even in well completions,
giving new life to work

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:11.690
that took place many years ago.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:14.330
Where physical and geographical
data are concerned,

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.990
Google Earth is the key
technology for visualization.

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.209
We can take the surface
view of the earth

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:22.250
and then expand it to
create a view of its

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:25.070
below surface features,
as well as we know them

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:28.790
from decades of our work in
exploration and production,

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:31.670
populating it with
information like seismic data

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:34.730
and well logs that
describe transitions

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:37.580
between geologic layers.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.040
We can add publicly
available information

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.920
about surrounding geologies
and industry activities,

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.900
developing still richer
ways of seeing our data

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.870
with new understanding.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:50.000
And this is really
literally decades

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.120
of human work seen in
the context of millions

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:55.590
of years of geology.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:58.160
And this is really just
the beginning for us.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.090
We have new ways of seeing and
using legacy and modern data

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:05.480
and linking it with
real-time business processes

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:09.320
happening within Chevron
around the globe each day

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:13.250
from earth science to
facilities engineering,

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:16.550
to environment and
safety, and beyond.

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:18.690
Through our collaboration
with Google,

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.600
we have developed
a framework that

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:23.540
enables us to do more
together, connecting

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:28.170
our technical experts to
our data and to each other.

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:31.070
And that's really made possible
by what our hosts today

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:32.770
are calling One Google.

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:33.540
Thank you.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

